title: My Favorite Books pt. 2
blurb: Three more I keep recommending, this time on sitting still, writing clearly, and the rules nobody mentions.
date: 2026-10-13
tags: [misc]
---

I enjoyed writing the last one enough to the list going. Here are
three more, and *gasp* none of them are about software!

## The Three Pillars of Zen

<p class="book-author">Roshi Philip Kapleau</p>

<img
	src="/books/the-three-pillars-of-zen.webp"
	alt="The Three Pillars of Zen, 35th anniversary edition cover"
	width="440"
	height="679"
	loading="lazy"
	class="book-cover"
/>

Kapleau was an American court reporter who covered war crime trials, went to
Japan, then stayed thirteen years to train under a monk. The book he came back with is not a book
about Zen, so much as a set of primary documents. You get a teacher's lecture series
on the actual mechanics of sitting, transcripts of interviews between the
teacher and his students, and then a looong section of first-person accounts from
people describing what happened to them. A student arrives with a tidy intellectual account of what they
have understood, and the teacher simply declines to accept it, over and over, until
the student stops performing. It is the least mystical book about enlightenment I
have ever read, which is a big reason why I find it so fascinating.

Takeaways:

- The posture instructions are not a warmup for the real thing, they *are* 'the thing'.
- A clean explanation of an experience is not the experience. Life is a messy business, and as 
Nietzsche once said about nature, there are "no exactly straight line(s), no actual circle(s)".

## Writing with Style

<p class="book-author">John R. Trimble</p>

<img
	src="/books/writing-with-style.webp"
	alt="Writing with Style: Conversations on the Art of Writing cover"
	width="440"
	height="646"
	loading="lazy"
	class="book-cover"
/>

Short, punchy, and the only style guide I have ever taken to heart. Trimble's premise is
that most bad writing isn't a grammar problem, but a courtesy problem. The writer
is busy expressing himself and has forgotten that a stranger has to follow his prose
sentence by sentence with no idea where it is going. So the book becomes mostly about the
reader: how to open without clearing your throat for a paragraph, how to carry
someone painlessly through a transition, and precisely where to put the thing you want them to remember.
He also spent a chapter killing the rules I was taught and never
questioned, like the one about prepositions at the end of a sentence. The advice
even holds up for commit messages and design docs!

Takeaways:

- Write the draft to find out what you think, then rewrite it for the person who has
  to read it. Those are two different jobs and trying to do them simultaneously really produces neither.
- If a sentence is hard to follow, that is *your* problem to fix, not the reader's to push through.

## 23 Things They Don't Tell You About Capitalism

<p class="book-author">Ha-Joon Chang</p>

<img
	src="/books/23-things-about-capitalism.webp"
	alt="23 Things They Don't Tell You About Capitalism cover"
	width="440"
	height="678"
	loading="lazy"
	class="book-cover"
/>

Twenty-three short, insightful chapters, each one opening with what you have been told and then
elucidating why it is false. Chang is a Cambridge economist and not remotely anti-capitalist,
which makes his points all the more salient. He is arguing that the version of capitalism most
people treat as the 'natural state of things' is one design among several, assembled
out of choices that were fought over, then forgotten. The opening chapter claims
there is no such thing as a free market, and the argument is simply that every market
in reality runs on rules you never even considered were optional, like who is allowed to work and what may
be sold at all. My favorite chapter argues the washing machine changed the world more
than the internet did, and he is annoyingly convincing about it. You do not have to
accept all twenty-three, but reading this certainly made me think about economic claims more carefully.

Takeaways:

- When something looks like the absence of rules, it usually means the rules are old
  enough that everyone stopped seeing them.
- The technologies that *feel* revolutionary and the technologies that actually
  rearranged how people live are rarely the same ones.

